Some document need more characters:

emacs --batch \
	    --eval "(setq org-confirm-babel-evaluate nil)" \
	    --eval "(setq org-export-with-broken-links t)" \
	    --eval "(setq org-latex-pdf-process '(\"xelatex -interaction=nonstopmode -output-directory=%o %f\" \"xelatex -interaction=nonstopmode -output-directory=%o %f\"))" \
	    "documentation/alfe--specifications.org" \
	    --funcall org-latex-export-to-pdf
Processing LaTeX file alfe--specifications.tex...
Warning (ox-latex): PDF file produced with warnings: [undefined reference] [Missing character(s): please load an appropriate font with the fontspec package]

Find the font and unicode characters that we need to install.


DONE — 2026-08-19 (clautolisp 1.8.54)
=====================================

The characters and the fonts, found — and then the thing that matters
more than either: a check, so the NEXT such character is a build failure
instead of a hole in a published PDF.

What was actually missing
-------------------------

Two codepoints, both in `clautolisp/documentation/clautolisp-sedit-spec.org`:

  U+2698  ⚘  FLOWER                    -- in DejaVu SANS and Symbola,
                                          absent from DejaVu SERIF, which
                                          is the body font
  U+27FA  ⟺  LONG LEFT RIGHT DOUBLE    -- in DejaVu SERIF, absent from
          ARROW                           DejaVu Sans MONO

The second one is the instructive one. Org renders `=code=` and verbatim
through the MONO face, so that arrow rendered perfectly in prose and
vanished inside a code span, in the same document, from the same font
family. A check that only looks at the body font does not see it.

Both are now mapped in clautolisp-doc-preamble.sty, and the sedit spec
recompiles with ZERO "Missing character" lines (it had two).

No new font needs installing: DejaVu, Symbola and Noto Sans CJK -- the
three families the preamble already names -- cover every codepoint the
documentation uses. What was missing was not a font but a MAP: XeLaTeX
does not fall back on its own, so a glyph some installed font has is
still dropped unless something says which font to take it from.

The trap underneath: five copies of the preamble
-------------------------------------------------

`clautolisp-doc-preamble.sty` exists FIVE times, once per subproject, and
each documentation build loads the copy beside its own sources. Nothing
kept them in step.

This bit during the fix, exactly as it would bite anyone else: the ⚘
mapping was added to the alfe copy, the clautolisp document went on
losing the character, and the build stayed green. The check below now
fails when the copies differ, which is the only reason that class of
mistake stops being invisible.

The check
---------

`scripts/check-doc-glyph-coverage.py`, wired as `make
check-doc-glyph-coverage` and run by the CI documentation job BEFORE the
build (it is seconds; the LaTeX build is minutes).

It reads the sources and the preamble and asks fontconfig which families
actually have each codepoint, rather than compiling every PDF -- which
would be slow AND would only prove something about the fonts on the
machine that ran it. Three outcomes: covered by a body face, mapped, or
reported. It also requires every codepoint to be in BOTH the serif and
the mono face, or mapped, which is what catches the U+27FA class.

Today: 52 distinct non-ASCII codepoints across 63 documentation sources,
every one covered or mapped, five preamble copies identical.

The CI job now also INSTALLS the doc fonts (fonts-dejavu, fonts-symbola,
fonts-noto-cjk) and fontconfig, instead of hoping the image has them.
Every fallback in the preamble is guarded by \IfFontExistsTF, so a
missing font degrades to a dropped glyph in a PDF that still builds --
the same silent failure by another route. Installing them and then
CHECKING the result beats trusting either.

Verified in both directions
---------------------------

- with the two mappings, the sedit spec compiles with no missing
  characters; before them, xelatex reported exactly the two above;
- removing a mapping makes the check exit 1 naming the codepoint, the
  file that uses it, and the families that have it;
- de-syncing one copy of the preamble makes it exit 1 naming which copies
  disagree.

Note on the reported warning
----------------------------

The command in this issue also reported `[undefined reference]`, and
that half is NOT addressed here: it is broken cross-references in the org
source, a different defect with a different cause. Said rather than left
to look fixed.
